[COMMENTS]
STATION,Velocity area station with informal flat-v style pitching strip. Natural flow regime. GENERAL: Velocity area station with informal flat-v style pitching strip. Station opened in 1965 in a 40m wide section between floodbanks, controlled by sheet piling d/s in straight channel which was dredged prior to station construction. Station was insensitive at low flows, so moved 50m downstream in 1997; sheet-piling removed and weir constructed. STATION HYDROMETRY: Insensitive at low flows. Multiple peak flow ratings applied across period of record. Suffers from weed growth in the channel and on the pitching strip control, with regular clearance sometimes difficult due to high water levels, and also gravel accumulation. Significant gravel removal in 2014 has improved section so that pitching strip operates correctly. Station is vulnerable to tidal influence since removal of sheet piling. Recent tree clearance to left bank will enable higher flows to be gauged, although difficult to gauge at high flows due to flashy response. FLOW RECORD: Pre-1997 peak flow data from the old station at a slightly different location is rejected pending review of consistency with the later data. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in October 2018 (WINFAP Files v7). FLOW REGIME: Natural to within 10% at Q95.
CATCHMENT,Impermeable Ordovician sediments with volcanic rocks outcropping. Tal-y-Llyn (southernmost ribbon lake in Britain) lies within catchment. Mostly grassland, with areas of upland heath. Some forest in S of catchment.
Qmed Suitability,Some scatter in the high flow gaugings.
Pooling Suitability,Station vulnerable to tidal influences.
[END]
